-- title:
--   Weight-zero cuspidal integrality forces vanishing
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $K$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, let $N$ be a nonzero natural number whose image in $K$ is nonzero, and let $G$ be an element of the intermediate field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,K\,N \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,K$, i.e. of the subfield of the field of Laurent series over $K$ generated over $K$ by the two series `jqModC K` (the $q$-expansion of $j$, namely $q^{-1}$ times the reduction to $K$ of the integral power series `jNum`) and `jqNModC K N` (its image under the substitution $q \mapsto q^N$). Assume that $G$, viewed as a Laurent series, satisfies `IsModPCuspFormFn K 0`, which for $m = 0$ amounts to two conditions: $G^{6}$ is integral over the $K$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,K$ generated by `jqModC K`, and there is a natural number $M$ such that $G^{2M} \cdot \mathrm{jqModC}\,K$ is integral over the $K$-subalgebra generated by $(\mathrm{jqModC}\,K)^{-1}$. Then $G = 0$.
--
--   This is the statement that a weight-zero form satisfying the cuspidality conditions on the mod $p$ modular function field vanishes identically: the two integrality conditions force $G$ to be regular everywhere, hence constant, while forcing strict vanishing at the poles of $j$. It is used in [`ModPForms.mem_modPCusp_of_mem_modPMod_of_isModPCuspFormFn`](thm.html#ModPForms.mem_modPCusp_of_mem_modPMod_of_isModPCuspFormFn), where the weight-zero case occurs at the edge of the weight range and the corresponding space of cusp forms must be shown to be zero.
-- source:
